mage repeatedly and watching which address steps exactly 6->5->4->3. Let me do controlled single hits. The issue is idling may cause multiple hits.\n\nLet me instead deliberately collide: walk into an Octorok and immediately snapshot. But knockback. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use a save state at full health (the .mss), and take exactly one hit, then compare with s0 (full health). Then take exactly one more hit from that save state, etc. But hits are random.\n\nHmm. Actually, an easier idea: Octorok rocks do 1 half-heart. When Link takes damage he flashes/invulnerable for a while, so only one hit per ~1.5s. Our idle 400 frames = 6.7s could include several hits. The 300-frame idle earlier may have had 1 hit. Let me check hearts progression precisely by reading candidate addresses after each controlled short idle.\n\nLet me look for a candidate: 0x0613. Let me read memory at 0x0613 now and compare with health on screen. Currently hearts? Let me zoom via get_pixel_color? Better: screenshot and count.\n\nActually, let me approach this differently and more rigorously:\n\nMethod: snapshot at full health (s0 = taken on start screen). Then take exactly one hit and snapshot, diff decreased from s0. Since start screen and this screen differ, many hits. Not clean.\n\nAlternative: Use the save state to reset to full health, then immediately walk into an enemy and snapshot right after the first hit. But navigation takes time and screens change.\n\nHmm, honestly the cleanest: identify the heart meter address by bisection with writes, using an event to redraw = taking damage. But we still need to trigger redraws.\n\nWait — actually the HUD probably redraws hearts every frame or on change. Let me test: write a value to a guessed address and screenshot. If HUD updates automatically, great.\n\nLet me first test whether RAM writes to HUD-related addresses auto-update. But we don't know an address yet.\n\nLet me get back to differential. We have candidate set d2 (46). Let's do another damage round and narrow. But we need the same \"decreased\" event. The problem is other stuff also decreased.\n\nActually, key insight: the real health counter decreases by exactly 1 per hit. If I take a controlled single hit and diff, the health address decreases by 1. Other addresses (timers, animations) may fluctuate. Let me try to get exactly one hit.\n\nPlan: load_state (fresh, 3 hearts, start screen), then walk to the enemy screen and deliberately get hit once. But that's ~600 frames of walking. Hmm.\n\nAlternative approach for hearts: use trace_writes to find code that writes to PPU during HUD drawing... complicated.\n\nLet me think about known-ish Zelda RAM layout from the partial mapping we have. We know 0x00EB = overworld screen, 0x0070 = link_x, 0x0084 = link_y. So the zero page holds player state and low RAM holds object slots. The counts/hud are probably around 0x0660-0x0680. \n\nNotice in d2 a candidate: address 1083 = 0x043B (250->248). Address 1555=0x0613 (5->1). Hmm.\n\nLet me do a targeted experiment: read a block of memory around 0x0600-0x0700 and look for 6 (health in half-hearts) or 3.\n\nActually wait. Let's reconsider: at start, 3 hearts. If health in half-hearts = 6. But maybe health stored as 3 (full hearts) plus fractional. Let's read the current bytes near 0x0660.\n\nLet me read memory 0x0600 length 0x80 and inspect."}
