{"type":"thinking","text":"This is gold. The reads at PCs around 0x9D2F, 0x9DB6, 0x9E24, 0x9F7F, 0x9E7B, 0x9DF9, 0x9DF6, 0x9D93 etc. read addresses 1553 (0x0611), 1555 (0x0613), 1558/1559 (0x0616/0x0617), 1554/1556 (0x0612/0x0614), 1563/1564/1565 (0x061B-0x061D), etc. These are all read every frame and their values count down (6,5,4,3,2,1) — these look like animation timers. Indeed 0x0611 and 0x0613 count down: 6,5,4,3,2,1 — so these are timers, not health. That's why my write got overwritten.\n\nSo the 0x0600 region holds per-frame timers. Health isn't obviously there.\n\nBut note the interesting reads: pc 0x9F7F reads 0x0619 (value 1); pc 0x9E24 reads 0x0616; etc.\n\nHmm, but these are all counting down, so they're animation/timer variables.\n\nLook for reads of an address with a stable value that doesn't count down. E.g., pc 0x9E1C reads 1548 (0x060C) value 89 stable. pc 0xED1B reads 1647 value 34 stable. pc 0x6D10 reads 1647 from saveRam. Hmm.\n\nThe heart routine: which PC reads health? The write PC for heart tiles was 0xA0D6. None of these read PCs is 0xA0D6. So the heart routine's reads aren't in 0x0600-0x06FF (with PC 0xA0D6) — or the heart routine computes health from something.\n\nWait, the heart tiles are written by PC 0xA0D6. What does it read? Let me trace reads on the full RAM but filter by pc. Actually, trace_writes returns per-pc groups; I could search for pc=0xA0D6 among reads of 0x0000-0x07FF. But length max 256, so I need 8 calls for 2KB, or target likely ranges.\n\nThe health is likely in zero page (0x00xx) or 0x02xx/0x03xx. Hmm.\n\nLet me trace reads on 0x0000 length 256 frames 4 and look for pc 0xA0D6.\n\nActually, better idea: trace reads on 0x0000-0x07FF but the tool caps length at 256. I can do 8 calls (0x0000, 0x0100, ..., 0x0700). That's a lot but doable. Or I can target zero page first.\n\nActually, wait — the HUD heart routine reads health. But maybe health is at 0x066F or 0x0670. Let me look at the trace output for reads of 0x066F (1647)? Yes! pc 0xED1B reads 0x066F (1647) with value 34 stable. And read_memory earlier showed 0x066F=33/34. Hmm, 34 decimal = 0x22. Not heart-like.\n\nHmm, and pc 0x6D10 reads saveRam 1647... confusing because \"address\" there is RAM 1647 = 0x066F, mem_type saveRam? The origin is saveRam? Odd.\n\nWait, the trace output mixes: \"origin\" describes the code's ROM location (mem_type_id 45 = prgRom, 48 = saveRam). The \"address\" in samples is the watched address (1647 = 0x066F). So code at prgRom 0x1ED1B reads 0x066F.\n\n0x066F = 34/33. Hmm.\n\nI need to find which code reads the health. The heart-writing PC is 0xA0D6. Let me trace reads on all RAM ranges and find pc 0xA0D6.\n\nAlternatively, trace READS but the writes of heart tiles happen at pc 0xA0D6. Let me trace reads on the range 0x0000-0x00FF (zero page) with frames 4, and look for pc 0xA0D6. If not, try 0x0600 already done — no 0xA0D6. Try 0x0300-0x03FF, 0x0200-0x02FF.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The heart tiles write values 242,101,102 = full/half/empty. The routine likely reads health (say H) and max (M) and computes. Maybe it reads a zero-page temp that was computed earlier from health. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the \"health\" is not in RAM 0x0000-0x07FF in a simple form but in save RAM.\n\nHmm, hold on. This is Zelda; SRAM at 0x6000 holds the save file. The status bar values might be read from save RAM each frame. The trace showed reads from saveRam (mem_type_id 48) at addresses like 0x066F? No — actually the samples' \"address\" field is the watched CPU address (1631-1662 = 0x065F-0x067E), and origin is saveRam code? That's weird: code executing from saveRam? The origin mem_type_id 48 = saveRam means the PC was in the 0x6000-0x7FFF range! Because 0x6000-0x7FFF is battery SRAM for Zelda? Actually Zelda has 8KB SRAM at 0x6000-0x7FFF. Code can't execute there normally... but the PC 0x7322, 0x6CCE etc. are in 0x6000-0x7FFF. So the game DOES execute code from SRAM? Unlikely. Unless the trace's PC reporting for reads is odd.\n\nHmm, actually maybe the trace records reads by the CPU including from a \"saveRam\" region meaning the PC was at 0x6000+... That seems buggy.\n\nLet me step back. The reads at 0x0600-0x06FF all look like timers counting down. Not health.\n\nNew plan: trace reads on zero page 0x0000-0x00FF for 4 frames, find the one read by pc 0xA0D6 (the heart drawer). Let me do that.\n\nLet me call trace_writes mode read address 0 length 256 frames 4.\n\nActually — wait. Instead of trying to find the exact PC, let me use a more direct behavioral proof. Now that I can trigger HUD redraw every frame, I can find the health address by brute-force bisection over ALL of RAM! Since writing a value to the health address changes the hearts immediately.\n\nBisection over 2KB with 256-byte writes: write a distinctive value to a 256-byte block, step, screenshot. If hearts change, block contains health. Then bisect within. But writing garbage to a whole 256-byte block would crash the game. Risky.\n\nBetter: use the \"reads\" trace to narrow. The heart routine reads health each frame. If I trace reads of each 256-byte page for 2 frames and look for a read with pc = the heart-writer PC (0xA0D6), I find it.\n\nLet me do zero page first.\n\nLet me trace read on 0x0000 length 256 frames 3.\n\nHmm, that trace output was huge. Let me instead think.\n\nActually — simpler idea: the hearts are drawn from a value; maybe the value is the one at 0x066F! Because 0x066F read by pc 0xED1B... but value 34. No.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe I mis-mapped the heart tiles. What if 242/101/102 aren't full/half/empty but something else, and the hearts are actually sprites? Let me check list_sprites at the heart location on the clean start. Earlier list_sprites at (180,52) gave 0 sprites (on death screen). Let me check now.\n\nActually, get_pixel_color at (176,50) was red (nes 0x16). Tile 242 background palette 1. That's the heart. Fine.\n\nLet me check: at clean, all three hearts are tile 242. So 242 = full heart. And 101/102 = half/empty.\n\nNow, which RAM byte controls it? Let me trace the heart-writer's reads. Let me trace reads on zero page.\n\nActually, better: let me trace WRITES of a *different* HUD element that's simpler: the rupee digit. Rupee digit tile at (13,3) = PPU 0x206D. Trace writes to 0x206D → get PC. Then trace reads of RAM by that PC. Same approach but rupees might share the same routine.\n\nHmm, but I still need the health's RAM.\n\nLet me just trace reads on zero page and look for pc 0xA0D6 (the heart/writer PC).\n\nActually, look at the write trace result again: the writer pc 0xA0D6, origin prgRom 0x160D6. The read trace shows code origins around prgRom 0x15D00-0x15F80 (pc 0x9D00-0x9F80) reading the 0x06xx timers. The heart write code at pc 0xA0D6 origin 0x160D6 is nearby (0x160D6). So the code region 0x9D00-0xA0D6 handles the status bar! And it reads the countdown timers at 0x0611 etc. Wait, those countdown timers could actually be the rupee/key/bomb counters being decremented? No, they count down every frame 6,5,4,3,2,1 — clearly timers.\n\nHmm, actually maybe those are the \"heart animation\" timers.\n\nBut importantly, the status bar routine is around pc 0x9D00-0xA0D6. Let me disassemble there — maybe the disassembler works now (the bank seems mapped since the trace ran). Let me disassemble at pc 0x9E24 or 0x9F7F, and at 0xA0D6.\n\nEarlier disassemble at 0xA0A0 gave data. But maybe now it's mapped. Let me try disassembling at 0x9E00 and 0xA0D6.\n\nActually, the read trace's origin_hex prgRom 0x15E24 for pc 0x9E24. And disassemble reads CPU memory. Let me just try disassemble 0x9E00.\n\nLet me do that. But the game is running; disassemble is read-only, fine.\n\nLet me disassemble 0x9E00 length 96. Also 0xA0C0 length 64.\n\nHmm, disassemble might also hit the bank issue. Let me try.\n\nActually, better: read prgRom at 0x15E24 and disassemble manually. prgRom 0x15E24 = 89636. Let me read 64 bytes.\n\nLet me do read_memory prgRom 0x15E00 length 96 (89600).\n\nLet me also re-examine: the code reads 0x0616/0x0617 (1558/1559) countdown. These are likely enemy animation. Whatever.\n\nThe status bar heart writer at pc 0xA0D6. Let me read prgRom 0x16080 length 128 (90240) — I did earlier, that was data (tile table). Hmm, so origin 0x160D","truncated":true}
