{"type":"thinking","text":"I found the status-bar routine's reads in zero page! Look at these:\n\n- pc 0xA0FD (0x160FD) reads 0x0000 (value 2, then 13, 24...) and 0x0001 (3). Hmm.\n- pc 0xA082 (0x16082) reads 0x0014 (value 0).\n- pc 0xA0FD group: samples value at address 0: 2,3,2,3,13,3,24,3 — addresses 0 and 1. So pc 0xA0FD reads 0x0000 and 0x0001. Those are link_x/y? No, 0x0000 changes rapidly (2,13,24) — that's probably a random/VRAM pointer.\n- pc 0x9827 (0x15827) reads 0x00E0 (224) value 0.\n\nNone at 0xA0D6 though. The heart writer PC was 0xA0D6. The reads at 0xA0FD (just after) read 0x0000/0x0001. Hmm, and 0xA082 reads 0x0014.\n\nWait — the status bar routine spans 0x9D00-0xA100. It reads:\n- 0x0611,0x0613,... (timers)\n- 0x0000, 0x0001 (via 0xA0FD)\n- 0x0014 (via 0xA082)\n- 0x00E0 (0x9827)\n\nHmm, but the counts (rupees/keys/bombs) and health must be read somewhere. Maybe they're read from save RAM (0x6000-0x7FFF)! Recall the earlier 0x06xx trace showed many reads with origin mem_type_id 48 = saveRam and PCs like 0x7322, 0x6CCE, 0x6D10... Those PCs are in 0x6000-0x7FFF. Weird, but the \"origin\" mem_type saveRam with address 4898 = 0x1322 — so prgRom-like offset within saveRam. Hmm.\n\nActually wait, I bet this is a trace tool quirk: it's reporting the *data* memory type of the read (cpu memory) incorrectly... no.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. In the 0x06xx read trace, there were entries with origin mem_type_id 48 (saveRam) and addresses like 27854 (0x6CCE). Those PCs are within 0x6000-0x7FFF. For Zelda, that's SRAM (8KB battery). The game wouldn't execute from there. Unless... this ROM has PRG ROM mapped there? Actually the iNES format: PRG ROM at 0x8000-0xFFFF, SRAM at 0x6000-0x7FFF. So PC 0x6CCE executing means the trace misattributed. OR the \"origin\" for those is actually not a PC but something else.\n\nHmm, this trace tool is confusing. Let me not rely on it further.\n\nLet me go back to behavior. Key realization: **the HUD redraws every frame**, so I can find each address by writing and observing. I need candidate addresses.\n\nLet me use the differential results more cleverly. For the health, my earlier diffs failed because health may not have decreased in round 2, or the health is stored in a way I didn't catch.\n\nLet me do a clean, controlled differential for health:\n- load clean (3 hearts = some value).\n- snapshot A.\n- Take exactly ONE hit (I'll do the navigation and stop as soon as hearts drop).\n- snapshot B.\n- diff decreased → health address (should be a small list if I minimize movement).\n\nBut movement changes many bytes. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: Find the heart variable by writing to a candidate and observing. I have a better candidate source now: the status bar routine reads. Let me look at what RAM the status bar reads. From the zero-page trace, the status-bar code (pc 0x9D00-0xA100) reads 0x0000, 0x0001, 0x0014, 0x00E0. And from the 0x06xx trace, it reads many 0x06xx timers.\n\nHmm, none of these look like health/rupees.\n\nWait, maybe the counters are in save RAM (0x6000+) and the status bar reads them. The 0x06xx trace's \"saveRam\" origins were reads of 0x06xx from code at 0x6xxx... I think the tool's \"origin\" mem_type for reads might be the memory type of the *data* being read? No, data was cpu.\n\nUgh.\n\nLet me try yet another approach: directly search for the rupee count by *changing* it. I can change rupees by... hmm.\n\nActually, wait! Now that I understand the HUD refreshes every frame, I can find the *rupee* address by a clever trick: the rupee digit tile is at PPU 0x206D. The status bar writes it every frame from the rupee RAM. If I could corrupt the RAM byte, the digit changes. But which byte?\n\nAlternatively, I can use the differential \"equals\" method with the shown value 0 and the fact that HUD reads it. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider the reads trace with a focus: I want the code that writes the rupee digit (PPU 0x206D). Trace writes → get PC. Then trace reads and find that PC's reads. Let me do that.\n\nActually, the heart writer PC was 0xA0D6 and the rupee digit writer should be nearby (same routine). The routine at 0x9D00-0xA100 handles the status bar. Its reads include 0x0000/0x0001/0x0014/0x00E0 and 0x06xx timers. Hmm. But where are the counters? Maybe the counters live at 0x0600+ too and are read by the same routine, but the trace showed only countdown timers... \n\nWait! Look again at the 0x06xx trace: reads at 0x0611 (1553), 0x0613 (1555), 0x0615 (1557?), etc. with values counting 6,5,4,3,2,1. And reads at 0x0616 (1558), 0x0617 (1559) counting down. These are all timers.\n\nBut maybe the counters (rupees/keys/bombs/hearts) are in the range 0x0670-0x0680, which I didn't see read in that trace except via \"saveRam\" origins at 0x066F (1647) value 34. Hmm.\n\nActually, in the 0x06xx trace, the watched range was 0x0600-0x06FF, and I see reads at 1644-1662 (0x066C-0x067E) with values 0, 34, 255 etc. Those are read by code with \"origin\" saveRam addresses. Interesting: reads at 0x066F (1647)=34, 0x0670 (1648)=255, 0x067E (1662)=0.\n\nActually the values 0x066F=34, 0x0670=255, 0x067E=0. Hmm.\n\nI wonder whether Zelda keeps the \"save slot\" data in SRAM and mirrors it in RAM. The status bar might read from a RAM copy.\n\nHmm, let me take a totally different and decisive approach: **find the health value by watching RAM bytes through a hit with play_input's watch.** \n\nTo get candidate addresses, use the fact that at clean, health is full (3 hearts). Let me consider that health might be stored as 0x30 (48) — recall 0x0610=48 on the tree screen. Hmm, but at clean, what is 0x0610? Let me read clean RAM 0x0600-0x0680. I have the clean state loaded? No, I've been running. Let me reload clean, pause, read 0x0600-0x0680.\n\nActually, at clean (after load_state earlier), the read at 0x0000-0x001F showed index 13 = 3. And search found only 0x0009=6. Hmm.\n\nLet me reload clean and dump 0x0600-0x0680 and 0x0000-0x0100, to compare with the tree-screen state. The health value at clean = 3 hearts.\n\nHmm, this is taking long. Let me think about the known Zelda RAM layout more carefully — maybe I can recall it correctly now that I see values.\n\nZelda uses RAM:\n- 0x0000-0x00FF: variables (Link X/Y, etc.)\n- 0x0100-0x01FF: stack\n- 0x0200-0x02FF: OAM shadow\n- 0x0300-0x04FF: object/room data\n- 0x0500-0x05FF: \n- 0x0600-0x06FF: more\n\nActually, I recall the Zelda RAM map (from the disassembly by z64):\n$0000 LinkX? \n$006C = Link's rupees? \n$006D = Link's keys?\n$006E? \nHmm! Wait. The zero page trace showed the status bar reads 0x0000, 0x0001, 0x0014, 0x00E0. And 0x9827 reads 0x00E0.\n\nHmm.\n\nActually, I recall now: Zelda stores:\n- $066F: Link's health (HP)?? \n- $0670: max HP\n- $0671: rupees\n- $0672: keys\n- $0673: bombs\nHmm, but 0x066F=34 doesn't fit.\n\nLet me just reload clean and read 0x0600-0x0680 plus 0x0000-0x0100, and see which values look like {health=?, rupees=0, keys=0, bombs=0}.\n\nAt clean: rupees=0, keys=0, bombs=0, hearts=3 full. So I look for a byte=3 (or 6) and three bytes=0 near each other.\n\nFrom the earlier clean read of 0x0000-0x001F: [136,143,12,14,0,0,22,255,127,6,8,0,0,3,0,0,0,1,5,0,0,209,0,0,126,66,190,59,71,49,191,220]. 0x000D=3, 0x0009=6.\n\nHmm, \"3\" at 0x000D. Maybe health=3 (full hearts count) at 0x000D? I tested writing 1 to 0x000D and hearts didn't change — but the game may have overwritten it. Let me check: after writing 0x000D=1 and stepping, the screenshot showed no change, but maybe 0x000D was overwritten back to 3 (e.g., it's a \"lives\" or \"hearts\" recomputed). Actually if 0x000D is health and the game redraws from it, writing 1 should show 0.5 heart. It didn't. So no.\n\nI think I should find the health via trace: the heart tile write PC is 0xA0D6. What does the code at 0xA0D6 do? Let me get a proper disassembly. The disassemble tool at 0xA0A0 gave \"dd f5 78...\" which looked like the bytes I read from prgRom 0x16080: [125,168,169,125,...] = 7d a8 a9 7d. Hmm, the disassemble at 0xA0A0 gave \"dd f5 78 79 7a 78 24 77 78 24 75 78\" — different from prgRom 0x16080's [7d a8 a9 7d aa ab 7d 80 81 7d dd dd f5...]. So the disassemble at 0xA0A0 reads a DIFFERENT bank than prgRom 0x16080. So the CPU bank at 0xA0A0 is not the one mapped at ROM 0x16080.\n\nThe trace said the heart writer PC 0xA0D6 has ori","truncated":true}
