atus bar recomputes 0x0312 from health each frame, so it'd be overwritten. Hmm, but 0x0312 changed exactly like the tile. It's a buffer.\n\nLet me test the h6d candidates: write a small value (like 2) to 0x060D, 0x061B-0x061D, 0x0670 and step, see hearts. But these may be overwritten each frame (timers). 0x060D 139->138 (−1) is a timer maybe.\n\nHmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe the health is stored in 0x0400-0x05FF. Let me do a quick snapshot of 0x0400-0x05FF now, take another hit, and diff. Link is at 2.5 hearts; one more hit → 2 hearts, two more → death risk.\n\nAlternatively, use the SAVE RAM. Let me read saveRam around 0x6000.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, in Zelda, the save file is in SRAM; the current game's stats (rupees, keys, bombs, hearts) are RAM at 0x0600-0x06FF? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the whole RAM now and find values that look like health=5 (2.5 hearts). Let me read 0x0400-0x05FF and 0x0000-0x00FF etc.\n\nActually, better: let me just snapshot 0x0400 length 512 (0x0400-0x05FF) now, take another hit, snapshot again, diff.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nActually, since the diff ranges are cheap, let me snapshot 0x0400-0x05FF AND 0x0700-0x07FF AND saveRam 0x6000-0x6200 now (post-ish), then take another hit, snapshot again, diff decreased.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nAlso snapshot 0x0000-0x00FF etc. already done.\n\nLet me snapshot:\n- m4_pre: address 1024 length 512\n- m7_pre: address 1792 length 256\n- sr_pre: address 0x6000 length 0x200 (512) mem_type saveRam? snapshot_memory supports mem_type.\n\nThen take a hit, snapshot post, diff.\n\nLet me do the snapshots."}
