n\nAlternatively, snapshot ranges directly: I still have w2_pre and w2_post (whole RAM). But diff covers the snapshot's range. Since the snapshots are whole RAM, diff covers whole RAM. To restrict, I could create new snapshots of sub-ranges... but I can't go back in time.\n\nBUT: snapshot_memory can take a named snapshot of a range. I could have done w2_pre as a range. I didn't.\n\nHmm, but I can run diff_memory with `from: wd1` and filter `unchanged`? That gives wd1 survivors that are unchanged in the second round — includes health if health didn't change! That's a way to separate. Actually, that's useful: run diff(w4_pre,w4_post, filter unchanged, from wd1). That returns wd1 survivors unchanged in round 2. Then health is in that set (if unchanged) OR in wd2 (if decreased again). One of the two.\n\nBut both sets are large.\n\nOK, let me just do the bisection over wd1. To make it efficient, I'll write to MANY addresses at once. Since most wd1 addresses are OAM/positions, writing 2 to them will glitch the screen but hearts should still render (HUD is independent). Let me try.\n\nRound 1: write 2 to the first half of wd1 (50 addresses). But I need the full list. The diff output showed 64 of 100. Let me re-run diff w2_pre->w2_post with filter decreased — the output is the same truncated. Hmm.\n\nI can get the full list by using `from` to exclude the 0x02xx region? No.\n\nAlternative: Use max_results? diff_memory doesn't have it.\n\nHmm. Let me just re-run diff with a filter that reduces: filter decreased AND... no.\n\nActually, maybe I can use the `name` and then chain: wd1 has 100. Run diff(w2_pre,w2_post, decreased, from=wd1) — that's the same. \n\nLet me think of a different way to enumerate: I can snapshot sub-ranges now and diff those? The state has changed. \n\nOK, let me just take a fresh controlled hit and snapshot SUB-RANGES explicitly (0x0000-0x00FF, 0x0100-0x01FF, ..., 0x0700-0x07FF) as separate named snapshots. Then diff each pair. That gives per-page candidate lists (each ≤ ~30). Then bisect within the promising page.\n\nThe hit is the hard part. But I can get one: load slot 3 (full hearts, Link at x100,y150) and play up 50 → hit (worked). So I'll redo that and take per-page snapshots before/after.\n\nPlan:\n1. load slot 3.\n2. Take per-page pre snapshots: p0_pre (0x0000,256), p1_pre (0x0100,256), p2_pre (0x0200,256), p3_pre (0x0300,256), p4_pre (0x0400,512), p6_pre (0x0600,256), p7_pre (0x0700,256).\n   That's 7 snapshots.\n3. play up 50 → hit.\n4. screenshot confirm.\n5. Take per-page post snapshots.\n6. diff each page (decreased).\n\nThis is ~14 snapshots + 7 diffs. Manageable.\n\nLet me do it. Load slot 3 first.\n\nActually, I realize I can be smarter: health likely lives in 0x0400-0x05FF or 0x0700-0x07FF (not yet covered). Let me focus: snapshot p4_pre (0x0400 len 512), p5 (same, 0x0400-0x05FF), p7_pre (0x0700 len 256), p0_pre, p1_pre, p6_pre. Actually just do all pages.\n\nLet me load slot 3 and snapshot pages.\n\nI'll do the snapshots in batches.\n\nLet me load slot 3 first.\n\nHmm, wait: slot 3 loads with Link at x100,y150 and full hearts. Good.\n\nLet me go."}
