{"type":"thinking","text":"At full hearts (3 full, tiles 242,242,242): 0x066F=34 (0x22), 0x0670=255 (0xFF).\n\nHmm, at full health, 0x066F=0x22 and 0x0670=0xFF. And at 2.5 hearts earlier: 0x066F=0x22, 0x0670=0x7F. Wait, earlier I read 0x066F=34, 0x0670=127 at \"2.5 hearts\". And now full: 0x066F=34, 0x0670=255.\n\nSo 0x0670 toggles between 0xFF (full) and 0x7F (damaged). That's a flag (bit 7), not health.\n\nAnd 0x066F=34=0x22 constant? Hmm. That can't be health (health changed).\n\nWait, maybe I'm misreading the address. The read is at 1632=0x0660, length 32, so indices 0-31 = 0x0660-0x067F. index15=0x066F=34, index16=0x0670=255. Hmm.\n\nBut the routine at 0x6D0D loads 0x066F into $0E and computes hearts from it. If 0x066F=0x22 at both full and 2.5 hearts, it's not health.\n\nHmm, unless the health is 0x0670? At full=0xFF, damaged=0x7F. Not likely.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I have the wrong address because the game uses a *different bank* of RAM at 0x066F vs. where the status bar reads. No, RAM is RAM.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the routine at 0x6D08 isn't the status bar heart routine; maybe it's the \"subscreen/inventory\" heart routine or the file-select. Hmm.\n\nActually — the memory at 0x0660-0x067F is in the save-data area. In Zelda, the save file occupies 0x0660-0x06FF? Hmm. The values there might be from the loaded save slot.\n\nHold on. Actually, maybe the health is at 0x066F but stored in half-hearts as a *BCD* where each nibble is a half? Hmm no.\n\nLet me just find the health by the correct differential now: compare full (slot 3) vs after one hit, restricted to 0x0600-0x06FF, and look. Earlier the h6 round gave 0x060D 139->138, 0x061B/0x061C/0x061D, 0x0670 255->127. And now we see 0x0670 255→127 is the full-vs-damaged change. So 0x0670 is a flag that changes on damage.\n\nHmm, but 0x0670 isn't health.\n\nSo where's the health scalar? The routine at 0x6E79 computes hearts from $0E (set from 0x066F). If 0x066F doesn't change... maybe the health IS 0x066F but the encoding is such that at full 3 hearts it's 0x22? Hmm: 0x22: whole = 2, frac = 2. The routine draws hearts using (15 - whole) and (15 - frac). Hmm.\n\nActually wait — maybe the health value at full is not 0x22; maybe the value 34 is stale because the buffer/status hasn't been rebuilt. But 0x066F is the SOURCE; it should hold the true health.\n\nHmm, unless 0x066F/0x0670 is actually a 16-bit pointer and the routine uses it differently. Let me look again at 0x6D0D: it loads $066F→$0E and $0670→$0F. Then JSR $6E79. In $6E79, `lda $0E` is used for the packed hearts. So $0E must be packed hearts. If 0x066F=0x22 at full 3 hearts... \n\nActually! Maybe the packed format is: high nibble = (hearts - 1)? or the value counts DOWN. Let me compute: the routine does `and #$0F` then `15 - frac` and `15 - (val>>4)`. So if val=0x22: frac=2 → 15-2=13; whole=2 → 15-2=13. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the displayed heart count = (val>>4) + something. At full 3 hearts, val>>4 = 2. At damaged, if val stays 0x22, then no change... \n\nI think 0x066F is NOT health. Let me look at the disassembly differently: maybe `$0E` is set elsewhere too, and 0x6D0D's `sta $0E` is for a different purpose. Hmm, but 0x6D0D directly precedes the JSR.\n\nOK, let me just find the health by tracing reads in the immediate vicinity of the heart routine. The heart routine is at 0x6E79, called from 0x6D17. It reads 0x0E and 0x0F (zero page), which were set from 0x066F/0x0670. So health source = 0x066F/0x0670. Trust the code.\n\nBut the value doesn't change with damage... unless the game updates 0x066F only when healing? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the damage halves are stored elsewhere and 0x066F is the *max* hearts while 0x0670 holds current? Let me check 0x0670: full=0xFF, damaged=0x7F. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually maybe the health is stored as 0x066F = packed (whole<<4 | half) but with a different convention where full = 0x2F? and my read of 0x22 is wrong due to... no.\n\nLet me do a careful experiment: load slot 3 (full), pause, read 0x066F. Then write 0x066F = 0x11 (1 heart?) and then FORCE a redraw by taking damage — no, damage changes health.\n\nAlternatively, force HUD rebuild without changing health: maybe the routine runs when you open/close the subscreen, or on a screen transition. Let me test: write 0x066F = 0x11, then walk to another screen (transition) and see if hearts change. Hmm.\n\nActually — the simplest proof: write 0x066F, then the game will rebuild the HUD when health changes. But if 0x066F is health, writing it changes health; the HUD rebuild might be triggered by the *damage event*, not by reading 0x066F. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether the HUD rebuilds every frame. The PPU trace showed the heart tiles written every 2 frames from the *buffer* 0x0310-0x0312. The buffer is rebuilt by the routine at 0x6D08/0x6E79 only when? Let me check: the trace of writes to 0x0312 showed 2 writes in 2 frames (one per frame?). Actually with 2 frames we got 2 writes: one at frame 0 (pc 0x6D08, value 36) and one at frame 0 (pc 0x6EDE, value 102) — both frame 0! So 0x0312 was written twice within frame 0. Hmm, that suggests the buffer IS rebuilt every frame (both writers in one frame).\n\nIf the buffer is rebuilt every frame from 0x066F, then writing 0x066F=3 should change the hearts. But it didn't! So either the rebuild doesn't use 0x066F, or my write got overwritten.\n\nWait, when I wrote 0x066F=3, I then read and it stuck at 3. And the screenshot was identical. Hmm, but the screenshot might have been taken before the buffer rebuilt? I stepped 2 frames. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the screenshot sha being identical is because the *whole screen* was identical — but if hearts changed, it wouldn't be. So writing 0x066F didn't change hearts. So 0x066F is not the heart source, OR the heart routine uses $0E/0x0F which are set from 0x066F only in a different code path.\n\nI'm quite stuck. Let me use a more reliable tool: **trace reads with the correct interpretation**. Let me trace reads on zero