{"type":"thinking","text":"Now this is revealing! Writes to 0x0660-0x067F:\n- 0x067E (1662) written every frame (value 0) by pc 0x6CDB.\n- 0x066F (1647) written at frames 7, 84, 237, 284 with values 33→33→32→32, by pc 0x7B8F and 0x7BA2.\n- 0x0670 (1648) written at frames 7, 84, 237, 284 with values 254→126→253→125, by pc 0x7B7D and 0x7BA7.\n\nSo 0x066F and 0x0670 ARE updated at damage events! The values at frames 7/84: 0x066F 33→33, 0x0670 254→126. And at frames 237/284: 0x066F 32→32, 0x0670 253→125.\n\nSo two damage events: at frame ~7 and ~84 (first hit: 0x0670 254→126, 0x066F 33→33), and at ~237/~284 (second hit: 0x0670 253→125, 0x066F 32→32).\n\nWait, but the values are written in pairs by two different PCs:\n- pc 0x7B7D writes 0x0670 = 254 at frame 7, then 126 at frame 84.\n- pc 0x7B8F writes 0x066F = 33 at frame 7, 33 at frame 84.\n- pc 0x7BA2 writes 0x066F = 34, 33 at frame 7; 33, 32 at frame 237.\n- pc 0x7BA7 writes 0x0670 = 255 at frame 7; 255 at frame 237.\n\nHmm, so the pair (0x066F, 0x0670) changes: (33,254) → (33,126) at frame 84? and (32,125) at 284.\n\nHmm, this looks like 0x066F and 0x0670 together form the health in a 16-bit fixed-point: value = 0x0670*256 + 0x066F? At full = 0xFE21 = 65057? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the health is a 16-bit value where 0x066F is the fractional byte and 0x0670 the integer byte, and each heart = 0x0100. Then:\n- full 3 hearts = 0x0300 → 0x066F=0x00, 0x0670=0x03. Doesn't match (33,254).\n\nHmm, but the observed values (0x0021 etc.)... Let me reconsider: maybe it's fixed-point with each full heart = 0x0100 and half = 0x0080:\n- 3 full hearts = 0x0300. Not matching.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the value is 0x066F + 0x0670*256 where full = 0xFE21 (65057) and after a hit 0x7E21 (32289). That's roughly halving. Hmm, and after a second hit 0xFD20? Hmm.\n\nHmm, this looks like two bytes of a *random number* or a running animation.\n\nHmm wait, actually, maybe 0x066F/0x0670 is a 2-byte \"health\" in a fixed point where the format is: high byte 0x0670 = hearts*... no.\n\nLet me reconsider the disassembly of the heart routine: it loads $0E from 0x066F and $0F from 0x0670. Wait no, it does `lda $066F; sta $0E; lda $0670; sta $0F`. Then the heart routine 0x6E79 uses $0E as the packed hearts byte. So $0E = 0x066F = packed hearts! And $0F = 0x0670 is used elsewhere (bit 3 test at 0x6E4C).\n\nBut the observed 0x066F values are 33 (0x21), 32 (0x20). Hmm. With packed encoding high nibble=whole hearts: 0x21 → whole=2, frac=1; 0x20 → whole=2, frac=0. Hmm, so \"2.?\" hearts. But the display showed 3 full hearts at the start!\n\nHmm, wait. At frame 7, the trace wrote 0x066F=33 (0x21). At that moment hearts were... The trace started when the game was already running (I resumed). So at frame 7, hearts might already be 2.5 or something.\n\nHmm, hold on. Actually, maybe the health encoding is: each unit = 1/16 of a heart? No.\n\nLet me actually determine the encoding by relation: the display was 3 full hearts when I read 0x066F=34, 0x0670=255 (after loading slot 3). So at full: 0x066F=0x22, 0x0670=0xFF.\n\nHmm, 0x22 = 34. If high nibble=2 (whole?) and low=2... \n\nThe heart routine computes (15 - frac) and (15 - whole):\n- full: $0E=0x22 → frac=2 → 15-2=13; whole=2 → 15-2=13.\nHmm.\n\nThen in the draw loop, it compares X against these. Hard to interpret.\n\nHmm, maybe the health encoding is: byte = (whole_hearts << 4) | (16 - half_state)? For full hearts, low nibble = 0xF? We see 0x2. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 0x066F/0x0670 is a 16-bit fixed-point health where:\nhealth = (0x0670 << 8) | 0x066F, with each heart = 0x0100? \nFull: 0xFF22 = 65314. /256 = 255.1. No.\n\nHmm. What if health = 0x066F where the value means \"hearts remaining * 16 + something\"? Full=0x22=34 → 34/16=2.125. Hmm.\n\nOr maybe 0x066F = number of quarter-hearts? no.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I mis-attributed; maybe the health is a 2-byte value at 0x066F-0x0670 and the game stores 0x066F=0x22, 0x0670=0xFF always at full, and after damage 0x066F=0x22, 0x0670=0x7F, then 0x21/0xFE (?), etc. The trace shows a sequence:\nframe 7: 0x066F=33(0x21), 0x0670=254(0xFE)\nframe 84: 0x066F=33, 0x0670=126(0x7E)\nframe 237: 0x066F=32(0x20), 0x0670=253(0xFD)\nframe 284: 0x066F=32, 0x0670=125(0x7D)\n\nHmm! The values look like a counter decrementing: (0x21,0xFE) → (0x21,0x7E) → (0x20,0xFD) → (0x20,0x7D). Wait, high byte alternates FE/7E and FD/7D. Low byte 0x21/0x20.\n\nIf we interpret as a 16-bit little-endian value: \n- 0xFE21 = 65057\n- 0x7E21 = 32289\n- 0xFD20 = 64784\n- 0x7D20 = 32032\n\nDifferences: 65057-32289 = 32768 (=0x8000); 32289-64784 = negative. Hmm, not monotonic.\n\nAlternatively big-endian: 0x21FE=8702, 0x217E=8574, 0x20FD=8445, 0x207D=8317. Differences: 128, 129, 128. Hmm! So as big-endian, it decreases by ~128 each step. And these are 4 write events at frames 7, 84, 237, 284.\n\nHmm, so maybe health = 0x066F*256 + 0x0670? = high byte 0x066F, low 0x0670. Full would be 0x22FF = 8959. After hit 0x217E = 8574. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the health is stored as a 16-bit value where 1 heart = 0x0100? Let's see: full 3 hearts might be 0x0300. But we see ~0x22FF. Hmm.\n\nHmm, what if 1 heart = 0x1000? no.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe these values are not health but the \"rupee\" or a score.\n\nHmm, hold on. The write PCs for 0x066F/0x0670 are 0x7B7D, 0x7B8F, 0x7BA2, 0x7BA7 — in the SRAM-mapped code region. Let me disassemble around 0x7B7D to see what it does. That code writes 0x066F/0x0670 on damage.\n\nLet me disassemble 0x7B60 length 96.\n\nThat should reveal the health update and encoding.\n\nLet me do it."}
