11 with 0x0670=0xFF: (1*256+255)/256 = 1.996 → rounds to 2 hearts. Display showed 2. And 0x22/0xFF = 2.996 → 3 hearts. So the display = ceil(total) or floor(total + something).\n\nSo actually the number of hearts displayed = floor(total_health) + (fraction>0 ? 1 : 0)? For 2.996 → 3 hearts. For 1.996 → 2 hearts. For a half heart like 2.5 (=0x0280) → 2 full + half.\n\nHmm, so health is stored as a 12-bit fixed-point number: integer part in the high nibble of 0x066F (bits 8-11) and fraction in 0x0670 (bits 0-7). Total hearts = ((0x066F>>4)*256 + 0x0670)/256.\n\nAt full 3 hearts: high nibble=2, 0x0670=0xFF → 2.996. Hmm, that's \"3 hearts\" only if the full value is 0x02FF and one more unit = 0x0300. So 3 hearts = 0x02FF?? That's 2.996. Unless the game's \"full\" is 0x02FF and a heart is 0x0100, so 3 hearts would be 0x0300. Hmm, 0x02FF is one less.\n\nHmm, alternatively the high nibble counts *heart containers minus 1* and the fraction adds. Ugh.\n\nActually, you know what, maybe the encoding is simply: \n- $066F = hearts in BCD-ish where high nibble = whole hearts, low nibble = sixteenths? \n- $0670 = sub-fraction.\nHmm.\n\nLet me just do a clean empirical mapping. I'll write 0x066F/0x0670 pairs and read the resulting heart tiles (full/half/empty tiles 242/101/102). That gives the exact mapping including 0x0670's role. And it's a solid proof.\n\nBut I need a rebuild trigger each time — the inventory Start toggle works. \n\nLet me establish the mapping with a few tests:\n- (0x066F, 0x0670) = (0x22, 0xFF) → 3 full (known).\n- (0x11, 0xFF) → 2 hearts? (observed 2 red hearts).\n- (0x00, 0xFF) → 1 heart?\n- (0x22, 0x80) → ? (2.5?)\n- (0x22, 0x00) → 2 hearts?\n\nHmm, this is a lot of tests. But for the deliverable, I mainly need to report the address and encoding. I can report the encoding as: \"current hearts in half-heart units\" if I can determine it. Let me determine it via the damage routine and one or two tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the encoding from the damage routine more carefully:\n\n```\n7b70: lda $0670\n7b73: cmp $0e\n7b75: bcc $7b90      ; if $0670 < $0E (damage), branch\n7b77: sec\n7b78: sbc $0e\n7b7a: sta $0670      ; $0670 -= damage\n7b7d: lda $066f\n7b80: and #$0f\n7b82: cmp $0d\n7b84: bcc $7ba9      ; if ($066f & 0x0f) < $0d, branch\n7b86: lda $066f\n7b89: sec\n7b8a: sbc $0d        ; $066f -= $0d\n7b8c: sta $066f\n7b8f: rts\n```\n\nand\n\n```\n7b90: lda $0e\n7b92: sec\n7b93: sbc $0670      ; $0E -= $0670\n7b96: sta $0e\n7b98: lda $066f\n7b9b: and #$0f\n7b9d: beq $7ba9\n7b9f: dec $066f      ; decrement $066f (whole byte)\n7ba2: lda #$ff\n7ba4: sta $0670      ; $0670 = 0xFF\n7ba7: bne $7bb9      ; (always) -> 0x7bb9\n7ba9: lda $066f\n7bac: and #$f0\n7bae: sta $066f      ; $066f &= 0xF0\n7bb1: jsr $eba3\n7bb4: sta $0670      ; $0670 = max\n7bb7: sta $0e\n7bb9: ...\n```\n\nSo $0670 is decremented by the damage. When it underflows, a heart is lost: $066F is decremented (which decrements the low nibble or borrows from the high nibble), and $0670 resets to 0xFF.\n\nSo $066F is a byte counting \"heart units\" where each heart = 0x10 (the high nibble is hearts, low nibble is extra?). And $0670 is the fraction within a heart (0xFF = full).\n\nHmm, so if $066F = 0x22 and $0670 = 0xFF → 3 hearts? Then each heart = 0x10, and $066F = 0x22 means 2 hearts + 2/16? Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait: when $066F is decremented at 0x7b9f, it decrements the whole byte; if low nibble is 0, it borrows from high nibble. So $066F counts in units of... 0x10 per heart? So $066F = 0x22 = 2.125 hearts? Hmm.\n\nActually maybe $066F counts in 1/16 hearts and each heart = 0x10: 0x22 = 34/16 = 2.125 hearts. Plus fraction 0xFF/256. Total = 2.125+0.996 = 3.12? Hmm.\n\nHmm, this is inconsistent.\n\nLet me try yet another interpretation: $066F = number of half-hearts? 0x22 = 34. No.\n\nOK let me just empirically map. Let me do a few tests and then report the encoding as observed. Actually, maybe simpler: I'll determine the \"current health\" encoding by testing 0x066F values and reading heart tiles. But this could take many rounds.\n\nAlternatively, report what matters: \n- 0x066F = Link's hearts (current), packed: high nibble = number of full hearts minus 1? and 0x0670 = fractional fill.\nHmm, not clean.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider: maybe at full 3 hearts, 0x066F = 0x33, not 0x2","truncated":true}
